# Notebooks for features that need help

A feature with a multi-step setup, or one that a reader understands best by trying it, gets a marimo notebook in
`notebooks/`. A notebook shows the feature working, and it lets the maintainer review the code by running it. Text and
a unit test cannot do both.

## When to add one

- Add a notebook in the same change as a feature that has a cornerstone role in a `ROADMAP.md` milestone.
- Add one when the user must make a choice with several options (a backend, a file format, a model), because a reader
  then sees the effect of each option side by side.
- Add one when the setup has more than two steps (keys, servers, extra packages), so the notebook walks through them.
- Do not add one for a small option or an internal change. A docstring example is enough.

## Content

1. Open with one cell that says what the reader learns and what they need (data, a key, a local server).
2. Run every step that needs no key or paid service first, and put each paid call behind a `mo.ui.run_button`. The
   notebook then works offline up to the first call, and a reader never pays by accident.
3. Show one example for each option the feature offers, with the same call in each section, so the reader compares
   them.
4. Show the failure and its fix steps for the setup errors a user can meet (a missing key, an unknown option).
5. Use only the public API of the package. A notebook that needs a private name shows a gap in the API.
6. Use polars for frames and altair for graphs (`libraries.md`).
7. Write the prose by `writing-style.md` (ASD-STE100).

## Register the notebook

- `scripts/build_docs.py` exports each notebook of `notebooks/`, so a broken cell fails the docs build.
- Add a page `docs/<name>-notebook.md`, with the same parts as `docs/notebook.md`: the purpose, what needs a key, the
  run command, and the iframe.
- Add the page to the nav in `zensical.toml`.
- Link the notebook from the guide of the feature.
- Run `just docs`.
